---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import ProjectFooter from '../../components/ProjectFooter.astro';
const description = 'An independently developed Unity puzzle game about shared controls, asymmetric worlds, and learning through playtesting.';
const levels = [
  ['Shared controls', 'Different walls and linked switches introduce the need to watch both worlds.'],
  ['Changing alignment', 'Teleportation changes the relationship between the two characters.'],
  ['Planning a sequence', 'Boxes, switches, and teleportation bring multiple actions into one puzzle.'],
  ['Reading the space', 'Different wall lengths create a safe position while the other character operates a trap.'],
  ['Combining constraints', 'Moving boxes in one world must account for enemies and space in the other.'],
];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head><BaseHead title="Shadow Runner — Chao Bian" description={description} /></head>
<body>
<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
<ProjectHeader />
<main id="main">
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">INDEPENDENT GAME DEVELOPMENT / UNITY</p>
    <h1>Shadow Runner<span>One input. Two worlds.</span></h1>
    <div class="intro"><p>A split-screen puzzle game in which every move controls two characters. I designed, programmed, tested, and released five levels built around the consequences of that shared input.</p><a class="button" href="https://jietiao.itch.io/shadow-runner">Play on itch.io ↗</a></div>
    <figure class="trailer"><video controls playsinline preload="none" poster="/projects/shadow-runner/trailer-poster.webp" aria-label="Shadow Runner release trailer featuring the two worlds and shared controls"><source src="/projects/shadow-runner/trailer.mp4" type="video/mp4" /><p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DW-bCynE808">Watch the trailer on YouTube</a>.</p></video><figcaption>RELEASE TRAILER / EDITED BY ME <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DW-bCynE808">Watch on YouTube ↗</a></figcaption></figure>
    <dl class="metadata"><div><dt>ROLE</dt><dd>Solo developer</dd></div><div><dt>ENGINE</dt><dd>Unity</dd></div><div><dt>SCOPE</dt><dd>Five puzzle levels</dd></div><div><dt>DELIVERY</dt><dd>Playable release + trailer</dd></div></dl>
  </section>
  <nav class="chapters" aria-label="Project sections"><div class="wrap"><a href="#mechanic">01 / Core mechanic</a><a href="#levels">02 / Level design</a><a href="#iteration">03 / Playtesting</a><a href="#release">04 / Release</a></div></nav>
  <section class="overview wrap"><h2>From an idea to a released game.</h2><div><p>Developed as a game course project, Shadow Runner took me through proposal, proof of concept, prototype, Alpha and Beta testing, and release. I handled game design, programming, level design, testing, publishing, and trailer production.</p><p class="credit">Art and music use external assets. My work includes selecting and arranging those assets to support the levels and their visual communication.</p></div></section>
  <section id="mechanic" class="section wrap">
    <header><p class="eyebrow">01 / CORE MECHANIC</p><h2>Same movement.<br />Different consequences.</h2><p>Shared input does not keep the characters in the same relative position. When a wall stops one character, the other can keep moving. The environment becomes a tool for changing their alignment.</p></header>
    <div class="media-text"><figure><video controls muted playsinline preload="none" poster="/projects/shadow-runner/core-prototype.webp" aria-label="Early graybox demonstration of shared movement and asymmetric walls"><source src="/projects/shadow-runner/core-prototype.mp4" type="video/mp4" /></video><figcaption>EARLY GRAYBOX / SHARED MOVEMENT, WALLS, AND LINKED SWITCHES</figcaption></figure><div><h3>Build the puzzle before the scene.</h3><p>I established the spatial relationships with simple geometry: different walls, paired characters, and switches that affect access. This let the puzzle structure take shape before the environment was dressed.</p><ol class="rules"><li>Move both characters with the same input.</li><li>Use different obstacles to adjust their positions.</li><li>Coordinate both worlds to reach the goal.</li></ol></div></div>
  </section>
  <section id="levels" class="dark"><div class="section wrap">
    <header><p class="eyebrow">02 / LEVEL DESIGN</p><h2>Five levels around one rule.</h2><p>The levels explore shared movement through different combinations of space, switches, teleportation, boxes, and enemies.</p></header>
    <div class="level-grid">{levels.map(([title, text], i) => <figure><img src={`/projects/shadow-runner/level-${i+1}.webp`} alt={`Level ${i+1}: ${title.toLowerCase()} across the warm real world and blue shadow world`} width="1280" height="720" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>LEVEL 0{i+1}</figcaption><h3>{title}</h3><p>{text}</p></figure>)}</div>
    <div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">A CLOSER LOOK / LEVEL 04</p><h2>Make the difference visible.</h2><p>The fourth level asks the player to use unequal wall lengths to protect one character while the other operates a switch and traps an enemy. The solution depends on noticing a small but meaningful spatial difference.</p></div>
    <div class="comparison"><figure><img src="/projects/shadow-runner/prototype-4.webp" alt="Level four graybox with unequal walls, an orange switch, a gate, and a red enemy" width="1280" height="720" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>PROTOTYPE / GEOMETRY ESTABLISHES THE PUZZLE</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="/projects/shadow-runner/level-4.webp" alt="Finished level four with stone walls and a barred gate in the two worlds" width="1280" height="720" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>RELEASE / ENVIRONMENT ASSETS REINFORCE THE WALLS</figcaption></figure></div>
    <div class="case-notes"><article><h3>What testing revealed</h3><p>A tester reported spending around fifteen minutes before noticing that the walls had different lengths. Another mistook the enemy enclosure for an exit. The spatial clue and the purpose of the gate needed clearer communication.</p></article><article><h3>My response</h3><p>I used environment assets to make the walls more distinct. The aim was to help players notice the information needed to reason about the puzzle. I do not have follow-up measurements of how quickly new players recognized it.</p></article></div>
    <details><summary>Watch the finished fourth level <span>28 sec</span></summary><video controls muted playsinline preload="none" poster="/projects/shadow-runner/level-4.webp" aria-label="Finished fourth level solution"><source src="/projects/shadow-runner/level-4.mp4" type="video/mp4" /></video></details>
  </div></section>
  <section id="iteration" class="section wrap">
    <header><p class="eyebrow">03 / PLAYTESTING &amp; ITERATION</p><h2>Feedback changed the decisions.</h2><p>Peer reviews on macOS and Windows helped distinguish unclear information, awkward interaction, and changes that weakened the puzzle itself.</p></header>
    <div class="iterations">
      <article><span class="number">01</span><div><p class="eyebrow">MOVEMENT &amp; PUZZLE CONSTRAINTS</p><h3>Remove a shortcut that bypassed the challenge.</h3><p>In the Alpha review, a tester found that Shift sprint let them outrun enemies in levels four and five and ignore the puzzles. A feature intended to improve movement had changed the conditions those levels relied on.</p><p><strong>Decision:</strong> I removed sprint and reduced enemy speed. The aim was to preserve the need to solve the puzzle while giving players more time to act.</p></div></article>
      <article><span class="number">02</span><div><p class="eyebrow">SUCCESS FEEDBACK</p><h3>Make completion recognizable.</h3><p>Players could solve a puzzle and still be unsure whether they had completed the level. I added a distinct completion sound and effects around the characters.</p><p><strong>Design lesson:</strong> Communicating success and helping players locate the goal are separate tasks. The added feedback addresses the former.</p></div></article>
      <article><span class="number">03</span><div><p class="eyebrow">BOX INTERACTION</p><h3>Smoother movement still needs recovery.</h3><p>An Alpha reviewer explicitly found the third-level box easier to move. A separate Alpha review still reported pushing it into a corner and having to restart.</p><p><strong>Design lesson:</strong> Better handling does not remove a dead end. Recovery from mistakes needs its own design attention.</p></div></article>
    </div>
    <p class="evidence-note">These changes combine peer review records with my release decisions. Later testing was not recorded, so I describe the intent of the sprint and visual changes rather than claim a measured improvement.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="release" class="release"><div class="section wrap"><div class="release-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">04 / SCOPE &amp; RELEASE</p><h2>Commit to the<br />core mechanic.</h2></div><div><p>Early ideas included changing shadow size through lighting and a multi-light boss encounter. I narrowed the scope because synchronized control already offered enough room for a complete game.</p><p>That decision let me concentrate on five levels, scene presentation, feedback, testing, and a playable release. Taking the project through publishing and producing its trailer was part of the work, alongside building the game itself.</p><p class="reflection">The strongest lesson: a puzzle must give players enough information to make a decision, and enough room to learn from it.</p><a class="button" href="https://jietiao.itch.io/shadow-runner">Play Shadow Runner ↗</a></div></div></div></section>
</main>
<ProjectFooter />
</body>
</html>
<style>
:global(*){box-sizing:border-box}:global(html){scroll-behavior:smooth;scroll-padding-top:85px}:global(body){background:#f3f1eb;color:#272b26;font-family:var(--font-atkinson),sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7}main{width:100%;max-width:none;margin:0;padding:0}.wrap{width:min(1200px,calc(100% - 96px));margin-inline:auto}p{margin:0 0 22px;color:#62675c}h1,h2,h3{font-weight:400;color:inherit}h1{font-size:clamp(60px,8.5vw,112px);letter-spacing:-.065em;line-height:1;margin:0 0 32px}h1 span{display:block;font-size:.48em;color:#7b7959;letter-spacing:-.04em;margin-top:18px}h2{font-size:clamp(36px,4.5vw,60px);letter-spacing:-.045em;line-height:1.12;margin:0 0 28px}h3{font-size:25px;letter-spacing:-.025em;line-height:1.3;margin:0 0 18px}a{color:inherit;text-underline-offset:5px}a:hover{color:#6c7845}a:focus-visible,summary:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #82965b;outline-offset:6px}.hero{padding-top:55px}.eyebrow,dt,figcaption{font:11px/1.7 monospace;letter-spacing:.08em}.eyebrow{color:#727c59;margin-bottom:28px}.intro{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:50px;margin-bottom:42px}.intro p{max-width:740px;font-size:19px;margin:0}.button{display:inline-block;white-space:nowrap;background:#30392a;color:#fff;padding:15px 23px;text-decoration:none;font-size:13px}.button:hover{background:#52603d;color:#fff}figure{margin:0;min-width:0}img,video{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:0;background:#171923}video{aspect-ratio:16/9}figcaption{color:#757a6b;font-size:10px;margin-top:14px}.trailer figcaption{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px}.metadata{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:24px;padding:30px 0 40px;margin:0}dt{color:#757e67;font-size:10px;margin-bottom:8px}dd{margin:0;font-size:14px}.chapters{position:sticky;top:0;z-index:5;background:#f3f1ebf5;border-block:1px solid #d4d5c8}.chapters>div{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:28px;overflow-x:auto;padding-block:18px}.chapters a{font-size:12px;text-decoration:none;white-space:nowrap}.overview{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1.2fr;gap:80px;padding-block:70px;border-bottom:1px solid #d4d5c8}.overview h2{font-size:38px}.credit{font-size:14px}.section{padding-block:90px}.section>header{max-width:760px;margin-bottom:45px}.section>header>p:last-child{font-size:18px}.media-text{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.4fr 1fr;gap:55px;align-items:center}.rules{padding-left:20px;color:#62675c;font-size:15px}.rules li{padding:6px 0}.dark{background:#232825;color:#f1f0e7}.dark p{color:#b9bfb1}.dark .eyebrow,.dark figcaption{color:#b2bf98}.level-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:36px 24px}.level-grid img{aspect-ratio:16/9;object-fit:cover}.level-grid h3{font-size:22px;margin-top:12px;margin-bottom:12px}.level-grid p{font-size:14px;margin:0}.case-heading{max-width:760px;margin-top:85px;margin-bottom:35px}.comparison,.case-notes{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:28px}.case-notes{gap:55px;margin-top:35px}.case-notes h3{font-size:23px}.case-notes p{font-size:15px}details{border-block:1px solid #535b4b;margin-top:24px}summary{cursor:pointer;padding:20px 0;font-size:14px}summary span{float:right;color:#b9bfb1}details video{margin:0 0 24px}.iterations article{display:grid;grid-template-columns:80px 1fr;gap:24px;padding:32px 0;border-top:1px solid #d4d5c8}.iterations article>div{max-width:830px}.number{font:28px monospace;color:#8b9477}.iterations .eyebrow{margin-bottom:14px}.iterations p:last-child{margin:0}.evidence-note{max-width:830px;margin:30px 0 0 104px;font-size:14px}.release{background:#e7e8dd}.release-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1.2fr;gap:80px}.reflection{font-size:24px;color:#404a36;line-height:1.5;margin-block:30px}.skip{position:absolute;top:-100px;left:20px;z-index:20;padding:12px;background:#fff}.skip:focus{top:10px}
@media(max-width:900px){.wrap{width:calc(100% - 48px)}.intro,.media-text,.overview,.release-grid{gap:30px}.level-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr}.intro p{font-size:17px}}
@media(max-width:600px){.wrap{width:calc(100% - 36px)}.hero{padding-top:35px}h1{font-size:58px}h1 span{font-size:30px}.intro{display:block}.intro .button{margin-top:24px}.metadata{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr}.trailer figcaption{display:block}.trailer figcaption a{display:block;margin-top:8px}.overview,.media-text,.comparison,.case-notes,.release-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.overview{padding-block:50px;gap:10px}.section{padding-block:58px}.section>header>p:last-child{font-size:16px}.level-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.case-heading{margin-top:60px}.case-notes{gap:16px}.iterations article{grid-template-columns:30px 1fr;gap:16px}.number{font-size:20px}.evidence-note{margin-left:46px}.release-grid{gap:15px}.chapters>div{gap:24px}.chapters a{font-size:11px}}
@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){:global(html){scroll-behavior:auto}}
</style>
